The mockup should no longer feel random.

It should clearly answer the problems identified by the assessment.

---

# Assessment Structure

The assessment should produce a single score:

## Business Modernisation Score

Score:

0–100

The score represents how effectively the business can compete online today.

It is **not** simply an SEO score.

---

# Scoring Categories

## 1. Discoverability

Measures how easily search engines and AI systems can understand and surface the business.

Includes:

- Technical SEO
- Metadata
- Heading structure
- Internal linking
- Schema markup
- Local SEO
- AI readability (AEO foundations)
- Content structure
- Entity clarity
- Search visibility

Potential integrations:

- Lighthouse
- Moz
- Ahrefs (future)
- Semrush (future)

---

## 2. Infrastructure

Measures the technical quality of the current website.

Includes:

- Core Web Vitals
- Mobile responsiveness
- Performance
- Accessibility
- HTTPS
- Security
- Broken links
- Technical health

---

## 3. Trust & Brand

Measures perceived credibility.

Includes:

- Visual professionalism
- Typography
- Photography
- Brand consistency
- Contact clarity
- Review visibility
- Social proof

Many of these will be proprietary heuristics.

---

## 4. Customer Experience

Measures how effectively customers can use the website.

Includes:

- Navigation
- Calls-to-action
- Booking flow
- Forms
- Information hierarchy
- Mobile usability
- Customer journey

---

## 5. Modernisation

Measures how well the website supports modern business requirements.

Includes:

- CMS flexibility
- Analytics
- CRM readiness
- Integration readiness
- AI readiness
- Booking integrations
- Review automation potential
- Overall platform maturity

---

# Shared Evidence Collection

The website should only be crawled once.

Collect once:

- HTML
- Screenshots
- Metadata
- Lighthouse report
- Technologies
- Navigation
- Forms
- Images
- Structured data
- Mobile screenshots

Persist all findings into one shared JSON object.

Every subsequent operator reads from this shared state.

No repeated crawling.

---

# Operators

Important principle:

> **Operator = one logical responsibility.**

Operators are NOT individual AI agents.

They are logical units.

Examples:

- Discoverability Operator
- Infrastructure Operator
- Trust Operator
- Customer Experience Operator
- Modernisation Operator

An operator may be implemented using:

- deterministic code
- Lighthouse
- Playwright
- APIs
- vision models
- LLMs

Implementation is irrelevant.

Only the responsibility matters.

---

# AI Usage

AI should NOT perform the inspection.

The inspection should be primarily deterministic.

AI should only be used after all evidence has been collected.

Its responsibilities are:

- Executive summary
- Business interpretation
- Prioritised recommendations
- Website redesign brief
- Mockup prompt generation

Think of AI as the narrator.

Not the inspector.

---

# Report Structure

The assessment should generate:

## Website Modernisation Assessment

### Business Modernisation Score

Overall score

Then five expandable categories:

- Discoverability
- Infrastructure
- Trust & Brand
- Customer Experience
- Modernisation

Each section should include:

- Score
- Evidence
- Why this matters
- Recommended improvement

---

# Most Important Addition

At the end of the report add:

## Why a New Website Solves This

This section connects every identified problem back to one solution.

Example:

Your current website is limiting your business because:

- Customers take longer to understand what you do.
- Search engines and AI assistants receive poorly structured information.
- Your business appears less credible than modern competitors.
- Mobile visitors experience unnecessary friction.
- The current platform restricts future improvements.

A modern website addresses these issues by improving:

- Discoverability
- Technical performance
- Customer trust
- Customer experience
- Long-term flexibility

Immediately beneath this section display the generated website mockup.

The report should naturally lead into:

> "Here's what your modernised business could look like."

---

# Design Principle

The assessment should never feel like an SEO audit.

It should feel like an explanation of **why replacing the website is a commercially sensible decision**.

Every metric should answer one question:

> **"How does a new website help this business compete more effectively?"**

If a metric cannot ultimately support the case for a better website, it probably doesn't belong in Version 1.

---

# Future Expansion (Do Not Build Yet)

This assessment architecture is intentionally extensible.

In the future, additional manufactured digital assets (CRM, automation, analytics, AI operators, etc.) may contribute to the Business Modernisation Score.

However, **Version 1 should remain focused on one commercial outcome: helping businesses understand why investing in a new website is the right decision.**